I don't understand...Linload's whole purpose is to load Milo, so I'm
not sure what you mean by you can read linload from CD but then "get
kicked into Milo."

Anyway, I don't know about Suse, but it isn't possible to boot RH7.1
directly from CD. You must specify  root= and it must be writable, so
the CDRom doesn't qualify. 

I was installing to an unitialized disk so there weren't any
partitions on the HD that could be used either. With no Linux native
partitions on the HD, you really *must* use a floppy with a ramdisk
image on it. With that available, issuing something like this to Milo
will start the install (ARC Syntax):

boot scd0:/kernels/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 

If the HD has an ext2 partition on it you might be able to use that in
place of /dev/fd0, but I've not tried it.

If possible, I also recommend using a DOS/Windows machine to create a
small (8M) DOS partition at the beginning of the disk, and copying
Linload and the appropriate Milo image to it. That way after install
you won't need a CD or floppy to load Milo from in order to boot the
system.

