    Known functioning Milo for XL266 attached.
    [My apologies to everyone for the binary]

3. Contrary to Mr. Davies assertion, the kernel supplied on the 7.1 
distributions is perfectly bootable from milo.

>Hitting any key, or allowing the countdown to finish both end in a
>system that appears to be hung.

I've never used the kernel on the floppy method, so I'm afraid I can't
diagnose what might be wrong.

You can't boot the kernel from CD with *only* the CD as available
storage, "root=" must be defined and it *must* be writable. Therefore
you need a Ramdisk floppy to boot the CD kernel if you don't have any
native Linux partitions available on the hard disk that can serve as
"root."

You can of course boot Milo from floppy, but I recommend temporarily
inserting the hard drive in to a Windows system and creating a small
(8M) FAT partition and copying linload, and Milo to it. That way Milo,
and later Linux, can be booted without the need for a floppy.

In any case boot to the Milo prompt, and with a previously created
RamDisk floppy inserted, and the RH7.1 CD in place issue the
following:

  boot scd0:/kernels/vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 

That should get the install started.

Here is another link you may find useful:

http://www.elia.ro/linux_doc/rhmanual/doc096.html
Alpha Miniloader Howto

