Hi,

Sorry for such a long message, it is a complicated problem.

A while back I posted a message about some of the difficulty we are
having with our Alpha Linux cluster (kernel 2.4.9, 264DP motherboard).
Although things are not as severe as they used to be, they are still
not very good. I would really like to know if anyone has seen the type
of problem I describe below, or if anyone has a suggestion for how to
figure out what is going wrong.

The basic problem is that our (dual processor) nodes run for a while
(sometimes for months) with no problems. Then all of a sudden, a CPU
is 'dead', e.g. the CPU remains 100% idle when you look at top and the
CPU no longer accumulates clock ticks in the /proc/interrupts RTC
entry.  Sometimes it is CPU 0 that dies, sometimes it is CPU 1.  If
the console is still working, we see a number of 'flush_icache_page
timed out, flush_tlb_page timed out errors' on the screen. I have
attributed these errors to the fact that a CPU is not responding.
 
After a reboot everything is back to normal.  The CPU failures appear
to be random.  They happen when the machine is idle, lightly loaded
and heavily loaded.  
 
A few months ago we had multiple failures per day in many different
nodes, and the failures occurred more often in certain nodes
vs. others.  Some nodes have never failed. The vendor responded by
adding fans to every node's motherboard (they feel it was an
overheating issue).
 
Since the fans were installed the number of CPU failures has
decreased, but they still do occur (about 2-3 a week) and the vendor
can't seem to understand what is going wrong.  Furthermore, since they
opened each case, removed the processors, installed the fans and put
things back together, everything has seemed to reshuffle.  Nodes that
were failing reproducibly no longer do, and nodes that never failed
now do.
 
We have had some theories to why this may be happening, what do people
think?:
 
1) The power supply is not adequate.  The manufacturer (API) of the 264DP
motherboard states that this motherboard requires at least 630 Watts
of power.  Our vendor puts three 230 Watt power supplies in each case,
